  all:{title:'A four-chambered pressure pump',text:'Select a chamber, valve, septum or papillary muscle. The model contains true segmented surfaces; great vessels and coronary arteries are covered in the lessons but are not separately modeled.'},
  left_ventricle:{title:'Left ventricle',text:'Forms the apex and most of the left border. Its thick myocardium drives systemic flow. The smooth aortic vestibule leads to the aortic valve; two papillary muscle groups support the mitral leaflets.'},
  right_ventricle:{title:'Right ventricle',text:'Forms most of the sternocostal surface. Coarse trabeculae and the moderator band distinguish its interior. The smooth infundibulum leads to the pulmonary valve and low-pressure pulmonary circuit.'},
  left_cardiac_atrium:{title:'Left atrium',text:'Forms most of the base of the heart, immediately anterior to the esophagus. Four pulmonary veins usually enter its smooth posterior wall. Pectinate muscles are largely confined to the appendage.'},
  right_cardiac_atrium:{title:'Right atrium',text:'Receives the venae cavae and coronary sinus. The crista terminalis separates smooth sinus venarum from pectinate muscle. The fossa ovalis marks the fetal interatrial communication.'},
  mitral_valve:{title:'Mitral valve',text:'Anterior and posterior leaflets separate LA from LV. Chordae and papillary muscles prevent systolic prolapse; they do not actively open the valve. Mitral closure contributes to S1.'},
  tricuspid_valve:{title:'Tricuspid valve',text:'Usually has anterior, posterior and septal leaflets. Its septal insertion lies more apically than the mitral insertion. Regurgitation produces a systolic murmur that commonly increases with inspiration.'},
  aortic_valve:{title:'Aortic valve',text:'Three semilunar cusps: right coronary, left coronary and noncoronary. No chordae tendineae. Its closure produces A2; stenosis imposes LV pressure overload.'},
  pulmonary_valve:{title:'Pulmonary valve',text:'Three semilunar cusps guard the RV outflow tract. Closure produces P2. Inspiration usually delays P2 through increased right-sided filling, widening physiological S2 splitting.'},
  interventricular_septum:{title:'Interventricular septum',text:'Separates the ventricles and includes a large muscular portion and a small membranous portion. The conduction system passes near the membranous region; septal defects and surgery can disturb AV conduction.'},
  papillary_muscle:{title:'Papillary muscles',text:'Contract with the ventricular myocardium, tensioning chordae to prevent AV leaflet prolapse. The LV posteromedial group generally has a single arterial supply, increasing its vulnerability to ischemic rupture.'}
