export const modules = [
{id:'gross-anatomy',title:'Position, surfaces & pericardium',category:'Anatomy',summary:'Orient the heart in the mediastinum and connect its relations to clinical findings.',objectives:['Identify surfaces, borders, grooves and major relations.','Explain pericardial layers, sinuses and referred pain.','Relate anatomy to examination and procedural hazards.'],sources:['anatomy'],lessons:[
L('Orientation and surface anatomy',`<p>The heart occupies the <strong>middle mediastinum</strong>, obliquely behind the sternum, with roughly two-thirds to the left of the median plane. Its apex points anteriorly, inferiorly and leftward; its base faces posteriorly. Do not confuse the anatomical base with the bedside “base” used for upper sternal auscultation.</p><ul><li><strong>Apex:</strong> formed by the LV; the normal adult apical impulse is typically near the left fifth intercostal space in the midclavicular line, with variation due to habitus and posture.</li><li><strong>Sternocostal surface:</strong> predominantly RV. The diaphragmatic surface is predominantly LV with a contribution from RV.</li><li><strong>Base:</strong> mostly LA, receiving pulmonary veins; RA contributes on the right. The esophagus lies posteriorly.</li><li><strong>Right border:</strong> RA; inferior border: mainly RV; left border: mainly LV and left auricle.</li></ul><p>The coronary sulcus separates atria from ventricles. Anterior and posterior interventricular grooves indicate the septal plane externally and contain corresponding vessels. The apex shifts laterally with LV enlargement, but an absent palpable impulse alone does not establish disease.</p>`,`An enlarged left atrium can compress the esophagus and contribute to dysphagia. Its proximity to the esophagus also makes transesophageal echocardiography useful.`),
L('Pericardial layers and sinuses',`<p>The <strong>fibrous pericardium</strong> is attached inferiorly to the central tendon of the diaphragm and blends superiorly with great-vessel adventitia. The parietal serous layer lines it; the visceral serous layer is the epicardium. A lubricated potential space separates the serous layers.</p><p>Reflections around the arterial and venous roots create two important recesses. The <strong>transverse sinus</strong> lies posterior to the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, and anterior to the SVC; surgeons can pass a clamp through this space to control outflow. The <strong>oblique sinus</strong> is a blind recess behind the LA, bounded by pulmonary venous and caval reflections.</p><p>Fibrous and parietal serous pericardium receive somatic sensation through the <strong>phrenic nerves, C3–C5</strong>. Irritation can refer pain to the shoulder. The phrenic nerves run between mediastinal pleura and fibrous pericardium, anterior to the lung roots. Vagal nerves pass posterior to the roots.</p>`,`A small, rapidly accumulating effusion can cause tamponade; a much larger chronic effusion may initially be tolerated. Rate of accumulation and pericardial compliance matter.`),
L('Relations and applied anatomy',`<p>Structures anterior to the heart include sternum, costal cartilages and intervening pleural/lung margins. Posterior relations include the esophagus, descending thoracic aorta and vertebral column. The diaphragm separates the inferior heart from abdominal viscera. These relationships explain why chest symptoms may be cardiac, pulmonary, esophageal or musculoskeletal.</p><p>Valve <strong>surface projections</strong> and <strong>auscultatory areas</strong> are different. Sound is heard best along its transmission path: aortic at the right second interspace, pulmonary at the left second, tricuspid at the lower left sternal edge and mitral at the apex.</p><p>Pericardiocentesis requires real-time imaging and an experienced operator: nearby myocardium, coronary vessels, pleura, internal thoracic vessels and abdominal organs are at risk. Learn the anatomical hazards rather than treating a landmark description as a procedural instruction.</p><p>Right-sided chambers are relatively anterior. Consequently, RV dilation may produce a parasternal heave and anterior trauma may involve the RV.</p>`,`An apical pansystolic murmur radiating toward the axilla suggests mitral regurgitation; the valve itself is not located at the apex.`)
]},
{id:'chambers-valves',title:'Chambers, valves & fibrous skeleton',category:'Anatomy',summary:'Internal landmarks, valve apparatus and the architecture of two linked pumps.',objectives:['Distinguish chamber interiors and their outflow tracts.','Explain how valve competence is maintained.','Locate the fibrous skeleton and AV conduction relationship.'],sources:['anatomy','valves'],lessons:[
L('Atrial architecture',`<p>The RA receives the SVC, IVC and coronary sinus. Its posterior smooth-walled <strong>sinus venarum</strong> is separated from the anterior pectinate region by the crista terminalis. The fossa ovalis on the interatrial septum is surrounded by a raised limbus. Small endocardial openings transmit the smallest cardiac veins.</p><p>The SA node lies near the SVC–RA junction. The AV node lies in the inferior interatrial septal region within the triangle of Koch, whose boundaries include the tendon of Todaro, the septal tricuspid annulus and the coronary sinus ostium. These relationships matter during electrophysiology procedures.</p><p>The LA is mainly smooth, receives usually four pulmonary veins, and has a narrow appendage containing pectinate muscles. In atrial fibrillation, impaired atrial contraction and appendage geometry encourage blood stasis and thrombus formation. Atrial enlargement indicates chronic loading but is not specific to one valve or rhythm disorder.</p>`,`A patent foramen ovale is a flap-like persistent communication after incomplete fusion of septa, not the same developmental lesion as an ostium secundum ASD.`),
L('Ventricles and the valve apparatus',`<p>The RV has a crescent-shaped cavity in cross-section, coarse trabeculae carneae and a smooth outflow region called the infundibulum. The <strong>moderator band</strong> crosses from septum to anterior papillary muscle and carries part of the right bundle branch. The tricuspid valve generally has three leaflets.</p><p>The LV has a thick muscular wall and a more circular cross-section. Fine trabeculae line its inflow region; the aortic vestibule is smooth. The mitral valve has anterior and posterior leaflets. Anterolateral and posteromedial papillary muscle groups connect via chordae to portions of both leaflets.</p><p><strong>AV valves open and close passively according to pressure gradients.</strong> Papillary contraction prevents prolapse during systole; it does not pull the valves open. Semilunar valves have no chordae or papillary muscles. Their cusps coapt when the arterial-to-ventricular gradient reverses during diastole.</p>`,`Acute papillary muscle rupture causes severe regurgitation into an unadapted atrium. Pulmonary edema and shock may occur even when the murmur is not loud.`),
L('Septum and fibrous skeleton',`<p>The fibrous skeleton comprises connective tissue around the valve orifices and connecting fibrous regions. It maintains the shape of valve annuli, provides myocardial attachment and electrically separates atrial from ventricular working myocardium.</p><p>The <strong>AV bundle</strong> normally provides the conducting bridge across this insulation. An accessory AV pathway may bypass the AV node, creating pre-excitation and a substrate for re-entry. The membranous septum lies close to this specialized conduction axis.</p><p>The interventricular septum is predominantly muscular. Its smaller membranous region has complex relationships to the aortic root and septal tricuspid leaflet. Perimembranous ventricular septal defects are therefore close to both valves and conduction tissue. Aortic annular disease, septal surgery and valve replacement may cause AV block.</p><p>Pressure overload favors increased wall thickness; volume overload favors cavity dilation with eccentric remodeling. Actual remodeling is influenced by duration, ischemia, fibrosis and loading conditions, not merely one geometric rule.</p>`,`The right ventricle is adapted to a low-resistance circulation. A sudden rise in pulmonary vascular resistance can cause severe RV failure without enough time for compensatory hypertrophy.`)
]},
{id:'coronary-circulation',title:'Coronary circulation & innervation',category:'Anatomy',summary:'Arterial territories, venous drainage, dominance and myocardial oxygen balance.',objectives:['Map LAD, LCx and RCA territories.','Define coronary dominance correctly.','Explain diastolic perfusion and cardiac referred pain.'],sources:['anatomy','physiology'],lessons:[
L('Arteries and dominance',`<p>The left main coronary artery arises from the left aortic sinus and usually divides into LAD and circumflex arteries. <strong>LAD</strong> runs in the anterior interventricular groove; septal perforators supply the anterior two-thirds of the septum and diagonal branches supply the anterolateral LV. The <strong>LCx</strong> travels in the left AV groove and supplies lateral/posterolateral myocardium through obtuse marginal branches.</p><p>The <strong>RCA</strong> courses in the right AV groove and commonly supplies RA, RV, inferior LV, the AV node and the posterior septum. Nodal arterial origin varies; the SA nodal artery commonly arises from RCA but may arise from LCx.</p><p><strong>Dominance is defined by the origin of the posterior descending artery</strong>, not by which coronary artery is largest. Right dominance is common; left dominance and codominance alter expected infarct territories. Therefore, ECG localization predicts a territory more reliably than it identifies a unique culprit artery.</p>`,`The posteromedial LV papillary muscle generally has one principal arterial supply. The anterolateral group more often has dual supply from LAD and LCx branches.`),
L('Perfusion, extraction and venous drainage',`<p>LV intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole; consequently, much LV coronary inflow occurs in <strong>diastole</strong>. A useful approximation for the driving pressure is aortic diastolic pressure minus LV end-diastolic pressure. Tachycardia shortens diastole, while elevated LV filling pressure reduces the gradient, particularly threatening subendocardium.</p><p>The myocardium extracts a large fraction of delivered oxygen at baseline. Increased demand is met mainly by increased coronary blood flow. Demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall stress. Local metabolic vasodilation, including responses to reduced oxygen availability, couples flow to work.</p><p>The great cardiac vein accompanies the anterior interventricular artery, the middle cardiac vein the posterior interventricular artery, and the small cardiac vein the right margin/AV groove. Most venous blood enters the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> and then RA. Anterior cardiac veins drain directly to RA; smallest cardiac veins empty directly into chambers.</p>`,`Aortic regurgitation can reduce aortic diastolic pressure while raising LV volume and oxygen demand: supply and demand may worsen together.`),
L('Autonomic control and pain pathways',`<p>Sympathetic cardiac fibers, predominantly from upper thoracic segments, increase rate, AV conduction, relaxation and contractility through β₁ signaling. Parasympathetic vagal activity slows SA discharge and AV conduction; direct effects on ventricular inotropy are less prominent than nodal effects.</p><p>β₁ receptors activate Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP and protein kinase A. This enhances calcium cycling and pacemaker currents. M₂ receptors activate Gi, reduce cAMP and increase potassium conductance in nodal tissue, hyperpolarizing cells and slowing depolarization.</p><p>Visceral pain afferents from ischemic myocardium travel mainly with sympathetic pathways toward T1–T5 spinal levels. Convergence with somatic sensory input helps explain chest, medial upper limb, shoulder or back discomfort. Symptoms may be atypical, and pain distribution alone neither confirms nor excludes ischemia.</p><p>Baroreceptors sense arterial stretch. Falling arterial pressure reduces baroreceptor firing, increasing sympathetic and reducing vagal output. Chronic disease and drugs can modify this response.</p>`,`Myocardial ischemic pain and pericardial pain use different afferent pathways; phrenic-mediated shoulder referral points to somatic pericardial irritation.`)
]},
{id:'histology-biochemistry',title:'Histology & myocardial energetics',category:'Foundations',summary:'From intercalated discs and sarcomeres to calcium cycling and ischemic injury.',objectives:['Explain electrical and mechanical coupling between myocytes.','Follow excitation–contraction coupling and lusitropy.','Connect oxygen deprivation with ATP failure and cell injury.'],sources:['physiology'],lessons:[
L('Myocardium at cellular resolution',`<p>Cardiac muscle cells are branched, striated and generally contain a central nucleus. Sarcomeres organize actin and myosin into repeating contractile units. The abundant mitochondria and capillary network support continuous aerobic work; they do not imply an unlimited reserve under ischemic conditions.</p><p><strong>Intercalated discs</strong> combine adherens junctions and desmosomes for force transmission with gap junctions for low-resistance ionic communication. Myocytes remain distinct cells but behave as a coordinated electrical syncytium. Fibrosis can interrupt uniform conduction and create arrhythmic pathways.</p><p>Endocardium includes an endothelial lining and subendothelial connective tissue. The subendocardial region contains Purkinje fibers. Myocardium supplies force; epicardium contains fat, vessels and nerves beneath its mesothelium. Purkinje cells are relatively pale due to glycogen and contain fewer peripheral myofibrils than working ventricular myocytes.</p>`,`Histology explains why scar can be electrically dangerous: surviving conducting bundles embedded in nonconducting fibrosis can support slow, unidirectional pathways.`),
L('Calcium-induced calcium release',`<p>Depolarization opens L-type calcium channels in the cell membrane and T tubules. A modest trigger influx activates ryanodine receptors on sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing a much larger calcium store. Calcium binds <strong>troponin C</strong>, shifts tropomyosin and permits ATP-dependent actin–myosin cycling.</p><p>Relaxation requires a decline in cytosolic calcium. <strong>SERCA2a</strong> returns calcium to the sarcoplasmic reticulum; the sodium–calcium exchanger removes calcium across the sarcolemma. Phospholamban inhibits SERCA when unphosphorylated. β-adrenergic phosphorylation relieves this inhibition, supporting faster relaxation and greater stored calcium for the next contraction.</p><p>Contractility is the ability to generate force at a given loading condition. It differs from preload. Increasing sarcomere length within the physiological range increases force through length-dependent activation—the Frank–Starling mechanism—without being identical to a direct increase in inotropic state.</p>`,`Positive lusitropy means improved relaxation. Sympathetic activation can increase both contraction strength and relaxation rate, helping preserve filling at faster heart rates.`),
L('Energy supply and cell injury',`<p>The adult myocardium uses multiple fuels, including fatty acids, glucose, lactate and ketones. Oxidative phosphorylation supplies most ATP, while the creatine phosphate system buffers rapid changes in energy demand. Substrate preference changes with workload, fasting, diabetes and heart failure.</p><p>Ischemia reduces oxygen delivery, limiting oxidative ATP production. Impaired ion pumps increase intracellular sodium and calcium; anaerobic metabolism produces acidosis. Relaxation and then contraction deteriorate before irreversible structural injury necessarily occurs. Restoration of perfusion can rescue threatened myocardium but may also generate oxidative stress and calcium overload.</p><p><strong>Stunning</strong> is persistent yet reversible dysfunction after transient ischemia and reperfusion. <strong>Hibernation</strong> describes chronic, potentially reversible reduction in function associated with inadequate perfusion reserve. Neither should be confused with established replacement scar.</p><p>Cardiac troponin elevation indicates myocardial injury. An acute MI diagnosis additionally requires a compatible rise/fall pattern and evidence of ischemia; myocarditis, severe tachyarrhythmia and other illnesses can also elevate troponin.</p>`,`A biomarker result is interpreted in clinical context. “Raised troponin” and “acute coronary thrombosis” are not interchangeable statements.`)
]},
{id:'embryology',title:'Embryology & fetal circulation',category:'Foundations',summary:'Looping, septation, aortic arches and the transition from placental to pulmonary exchange.',objectives:['Trace major derivatives of the primitive heart tube.','Relate septation defects to congenital lesions.','Explain the three fetal shunts and changes at birth.'],sources:['embryology'],lessons:[
L('Tube formation and looping',`<p>Cardiac progenitors arise in splanchnic lateral plate mesoderm. Embryonic folding brings paired endocardial tubes together. The early tube has inflow, atrial, ventricular and outflow components; coordinated elongation and rightward looping establish the spatial arrangement of the future chambers.</p><p>The primitive ventricle contributes predominantly to trabeculated LV, while proximal bulbus cordis contributes predominantly to trabeculated RV. Conus cordis contributes to ventricular outflow tracts; truncus arteriosus contributes to the proximal great arterial outflow. Primitive atrial tissue contributes to trabeculated atrial/appendage regions.</p><p>The right sinus venosus horn is incorporated into the smooth RA; the left horn contributes to coronary sinus. Incorporation of pulmonary venous tissue is associated with development of the smooth LA. Development is a process of growth, remodeling and tissue contributions, rather than a rigid one-segment-to-one-adult-part map.</p>`,`Abnormal looping and laterality can rearrange chamber connections. Always describe atrial arrangement, ventricular morphology and great artery connections separately.`),
L('Septation and outflow development',`<p>Septum primum descends toward endocardial cushions, narrowing ostium primum. Before this closes, perforations form ostium secundum. Septum secundum develops to the right, leaving the foramen ovale; septum primum acts as its flap valve. The arrangement supports right-to-left fetal atrial flow.</p><p>Ventricular septation combines muscular growth with formation of the membranous region near the cushions and outflow septum. Endocardial cushions participate in AV canal partitioning and valve/septal development. Defective cushion development can produce AV septal defects, associated especially with trisomy 21.</p><p>Neural crest and other outflow progenitors contribute to conotruncal development. Abnormal outflow alignment/septation underlies lesions such as tetralogy of Fallot and persistent truncus arteriosus. In transposition of the great arteries, ventricular–arterial connections are discordant, creating parallel rather than serial circulations.</p>`,`A cyanotic newborn with parallel circulations depends on mixing through an atrial/ventricular communication or patent ductus; the anatomical connection determines the physiology.`),
L('Fetal shunts and transition at birth',`<p>Oxygenated placental blood enters through the umbilical vein. Some passes through the liver; some bypasses it through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to IVC. Preferential streaming directs relatively oxygen-rich blood through the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> toward LA, LV and ascending aorta. Much RV output bypasses high-resistance fetal lungs through the <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into descending aorta.</p><p>At birth, ventilation lowers pulmonary vascular resistance and increases pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placenta and increases systemic resistance. LA pressure exceeds RA pressure, functionally closing the foramen ovale. Increased oxygen tension and reduced prostaglandin signaling promote ductal constriction. Functional closure precedes anatomical obliteration.</p><p>Adult remnants include ligamentum venosum, ligamentum arteriosum and ligamentum teres hepatis. Fetal flow patterns should be understood through pressure and resistance, not memorized merely as arrows.</p>`,`Prostaglandin E₁ can maintain ductal patency in selected duct-dependent congenital lesions under specialist monitoring. A PDA is not always something to close immediately.`)
]},
{id:'electrophysiology',title:'Action potentials & conduction',category:'Physiology',summary:'Fast and slow responses, refractory periods and mechanisms of arrhythmogenesis.',objectives:['Compare nodal and ventricular action potentials.','Explain AV delay and conduction velocity.','Distinguish automaticity, triggered activity and re-entry.'],sources:['physiology','ecg'],lessons:[
L('Ventricular action potential',`<p><strong>Phase 0:</strong> rapid opening of fast sodium channels produces the upstroke. Its slope affects conduction velocity. <strong>Phase 1:</strong> sodium inactivation and transient outward potassium current cause early repolarization. <strong>Phase 2:</strong> inward L-type calcium current balances outward potassium currents, producing the plateau and supporting contraction.</p><p><strong>Phase 3:</strong> calcium current declines while potassium currents dominate, returning the membrane toward its resting potential. <strong>Phase 4:</strong> inward rectifier potassium conductance stabilizes the negative resting potential in working ventricular cells. These phases describe a cell membrane voltage, not a surface ECG waveform.</p><p>Most sodium channels cannot immediately reactivate after depolarization. The effective refractory period prevents propagated premature responses and normally prevents tetanic contraction. A sufficiently late premature impulse may conduct heterogeneously, particularly in diseased tissue.</p>`,`Class I agents alter fast-channel conduction; class III agents often prolong repolarization. A drug’s clinical effects cannot be predicted from its class alone.`),
L('Pacemaker cells and propagation',`<p>SA and AV nodal cells do not have a stable phase 4 resting potential. Hyperpolarization-activated “funny” current, changing potassium currents and calcium entry contribute to spontaneous diastolic depolarization. Their phase 0 upstroke depends primarily on <strong>L-type calcium current</strong>, making nodal conduction slower than fast-response tissue.</p><p>The SA node normally leads because it has the highest intrinsic pacemaker frequency. Atrial activation reaches the AV node, where slow conduction creates time for atrial emptying before ventricular systole. Impulses pass through His bundle, right/left bundle branches and the rapid Purkinje network.</p><p>Sympathetic activation steepens pacemaker depolarization and accelerates AV conduction. Vagal activity lowers cAMP and increases potassium conductance, slowing both. The PR interval includes atrial and AV conduction; it is not a direct isolated measurement of AV nodal time.</p>`,`Adenosine transiently suppresses AV nodal conduction. This may terminate AV-node-dependent re-entry but does not imply it treats every tachyarrhythmia.`),
L('Why abnormal rhythms occur',`<p><strong>Enhanced automaticity</strong> is excessive spontaneous impulse generation. <strong>Triggered activity</strong> depends on a preceding action potential: early afterdepolarizations occur before repolarization completes and are associated with prolonged repolarization; delayed afterdepolarizations occur afterward and are often linked to intracellular calcium overload.</p><p><strong>Re-entry</strong> requires a circuit, unidirectional block and sufficiently slow conduction that previously refractory tissue recovers before the impulse returns. Scar-related ventricular tachycardia, AV nodal re-entry and accessory-pathway re-entry are examples with different anatomical substrates.</p><p>Conduction velocity and refractory period determine excitation wavelength. Heterogeneous changes can make re-entry more likely even if a drug slows the rhythm initially. Electrolytes, ischemia, autonomic state and drugs all modify these properties.</p><p>QT prolongation is one substrate for torsades de pointes, especially with bradycardia and electrolyte abnormalities. A wide QRS caused by delayed depolarization also lengthens QT, complicating interpretation.</p>`,`Distinguish an early afterdepolarization during phases 2–3 from a delayed afterdepolarization after repolarization. Their triggers and therapeutic implications differ.`)
]},
{id:'cardiac-cycle',title:'Cardiac cycle & heart sounds',category:'Physiology',summary:'Unify pressure, volume, valve movement, the ECG and auscultation.',objectives:['Determine valve state from pressure gradients.','Align the Wiggers diagram with S1 and S2.','Explain S3, S4 and splitting of S2.'],sources:['cycle','physiology'],lessons:[
L('Pressure determines the phases',`<p>Start at end diastole: the LV has reached EDV. Ventricular depolarization precedes mechanical contraction. Once LV pressure exceeds LA pressure, the mitral valve closes. Both valves remain closed during <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>; pressure rises at constant volume.</p><p>When LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and ejection begins. LV volume falls toward ESV. As contraction declines and the arterial-to-ventricular gradient reverses, the aortic valve closes. <strong>Isovolumetric relaxation</strong> then lowers LV pressure at constant ESV. When LV pressure falls below LA pressure, the mitral valve opens and filling resumes.</p><p>Filling comprises rapid early filling, slower diastasis and late atrial contribution. The importance of atrial contraction rises when ventricular relaxation is impaired or compliance is reduced. Tachycardia disproportionately shortens diastolic time.</p>`,`Valves do not open because a timer reaches “systole.” They respond to instantaneous pressure gradients. This is why valve disease changes the normal phase relationships.`),
L('Wiggers diagram and pressure waves',`<p>A Wiggers diagram aligns ECG, chamber/arterial pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds on a common time axis. P represents atrial depolarization, QRS ventricular depolarization and T ventricular repolarization. Mechanical events follow electrical activation with a delay.</p><p>The atrial pressure <strong>a wave</strong> reflects atrial contraction. The <strong>c wave</strong> is associated with bulging of the closed AV valve during early ventricular contraction. The <strong>v wave</strong> reflects atrial filling while the AV valve is closed. The x descent accompanies atrial relaxation and downward displacement of the AV plane; y descent accompanies atrial emptying after AV opening.</p><p>Aortic valve closure is associated with the dicrotic notch in the aortic pressure trace. Atrial fibrillation removes organized a waves; AV dissociation may produce cannon a waves when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve. Large v waves can occur in AV valve regurgitation but require contextual interpretation.</p>`,`A venous waveform is a record of pressure transmission, not a direct tracing of chamber volume. Link each deflection to the responsible mechanical event.`),
L('Sounds, splitting and gallops',`<p><strong>S1</strong> accompanies closure-related vibration of the AV valve apparatus near the start of systole. <strong>S2</strong> accompanies semilunar closure. Inspiration usually widens physiological splitting of S2, mainly through delayed pulmonary closure. Aortic closure normally precedes pulmonary closure.</p><p>Fixed wide splitting suggests ASD physiology. Wide variable splitting may occur with delayed RV emptying or right bundle branch block. Paradoxical splitting occurs when A2 is delayed, as in left bundle branch block or severe aortic outflow obstruction, with splitting more evident during expiration.</p><p><strong>S3</strong> occurs in early diastole during rapid filling. It may be physiological in younger people but in an older symptomatic adult suggests volume loading or ventricular dysfunction. <strong>S4</strong> occurs with atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle and requires effective atrial systole; it is absent in atrial fibrillation.</p>`,`S3 is a filling-associated sound; S4 is an atrial-contraction-associated sound. Neither is caused simply by “a valve opening.”`)
]},
{id:'hemodynamics',title:'Hemodynamics & pressure–volume loops',category:'Physiology',summary:'Preload, afterload, contractility, compliance and ventricular–arterial coupling.',objectives:['Calculate SV, EF and cardiac output with units.','Predict PV loop changes from loading and inotropy.','Use resistance, wall stress and oxygen delivery relationships.'],sources:['physiology','cycle'],lessons:[
L('Quantities and definitions',`<p><strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>; <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>. Convert mL/min to L/min by dividing by 1,000. <strong>EF = SV / EDV × 100%</strong>. Cardiac index divides CO by body surface area; this distinction matters when comparing patients of different size.</p><p>Preload is myocardial stretch/load before contraction; EDV and end-diastolic pressure are related surrogates, not interchangeable definitions. The pressure corresponding to a given volume depends on compliance. Afterload is the load opposing fiber shortening, influenced by arterial pressure, vascular properties, valve obstruction and ventricular geometry.</p><p>Contractility describes load-independent inotropic state conceptually; no single bedside measurement is perfectly load-independent. Ejection fraction changes with preload and afterload as well as intrinsic function. A normal EF does not exclude heart failure.</p><p>Mean arterial pressure is approximated as diastolic pressure plus one-third pulse pressure at ordinary rates, but the coefficient changes with waveform and heart rate.</p>`,`An EF of 60% from an EDV of 70 mL gives a stroke volume of 42 mL. A reassuring-looking fraction can coexist with low forward flow.`),
L('Read and alter a PV loop',`<p>Plot LV pressure vertically and LV volume horizontally. The counterclockwise loop proceeds through filling, isovolumetric contraction, ejection and isovolumetric relaxation. Loop width is stroke volume; enclosed area approximates external stroke work when pressure and volume units are converted appropriately.</p><p>Increasing preload with other properties held constant generally increases EDV and SV. Increasing afterload acutely increases ESV and reduces SV. Increasing contractility steepens the end-systolic pressure–volume relationship, reducing ESV at a given loading condition. Reduced compliance shifts the end-diastolic pressure–volume relationship upward/leftward.</p><p>The lab uses <strong>P<sub>es</sub> = E<sub>es</sub>(V<sub>es</sub> − V₀)</strong> and <strong>P<sub>es</sub> = E<sub>a</sub> × SV</strong>. Solving gives V<sub>es</sub> = (E<sub>a</sub> × EDV + E<sub>es</sub> × V₀)/(E<sub>a</sub> + E<sub>es</sub>). This is a simplified coupling model: changing heart rate alone changes computed CO without modeling altered filling or reflexes.</p>`,`Regurgitant valves eliminate strictly isovolumetric phases. The normal-valve simulator must not be used to infer regurgitant PV loops.`),
L('Flow, resistance and oxygen delivery',`<p><strong>Flow = pressure difference / resistance.</strong> Systemic vascular resistance is often expressed as 80 × (MAP − RAP)/CO in dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ when pressure is in mmHg and output in L/min. Poiseuille’s relationship highlights the fourth-power influence of radius under ideal laminar tube-flow assumptions; living pulsatile circulation is more complex.</p><p>Laplace-type relationships link wall stress to pressure and chamber radius, and inversely to wall thickness. These are geometric approximations: a dilated ventricle may need more myocardial tension to generate the same pressure.</p><p>Oxygen delivery equals CO × arterial oxygen content. Content is dominated by hemoglobin-bound oxygen, approximately 1.34 × Hb × saturation, with a small dissolved component. Unit conversion matters: content in mL/dL requires a factor of 10 when output is in L/min.</p><p>The Fick principle estimates flow as oxygen consumption divided by the arterial–mixed venous oxygen content difference. It depends on reliable sampling and steady-state assumptions.</p>`,`Normal oxygen saturation does not guarantee normal oxygen delivery: severe anemia or low cardiac output can reduce delivery despite a normal pulse oximeter reading.`)
]},
{id:'ecg-interpretation',title:'The 12-lead ECG, systematically',category:'Clinical',summary:'Calibration, axis, intervals, localization and common interpretation traps.',objectives:['Apply a reproducible ECG-reading sequence.','Localize electrical changes using contiguous leads.','Recognize limitations of schematic and single-lead traces.'],sources:['ecg'],lessons:[
L('Calibration, rate and axis',`<p>Before interpretation, verify patient identity, date/time, calibration, lead placement and artifact. At <strong>25 mm/s</strong>, a small horizontal square is 40 ms and a large square is 200 ms. At <strong>10 mm/mV</strong>, a vertical millimeter is 0.1 mV. Paper speed and gain must be checked rather than assumed.</p><p>For a regular rhythm, rate is 300 divided by large squares between R waves, or 1,500 divided by small squares. For irregular rhythms, count QRS complexes over a known time interval. Examine P-wave morphology and its relationship to each QRS before calling a rhythm sinus.</p><p>Assess frontal QRS axis using leads I and aVF; lead II helps resolve the borderline leftward quadrant. A normal adult axis is commonly about −30° to +90°, with contextual variation. Limb-lead reversal can simulate abnormal axis and ischemic patterns.</p><p>Proceed through rate, rhythm, axis, intervals, P waves, QRS morphology/R-wave progression, ST segments and T waves, then compare with prior recordings.</p>`,`A computer-generated ECG interpretation is a starting hypothesis. Verify rhythm, intervals and ischemic findings yourself.`),
L('Intervals and regional views',`<p>The <strong>PR interval</strong>, usually 120–200 ms in adults, extends from P onset to QRS onset. A prolonged PR suggests delayed AV conduction. A short PR with a delta wave and widened QRS suggests ventricular pre-excitation. QRS duration is normally under 120 ms; widening can result from bundle block, ventricular origin, pacing, drugs or metabolic disturbance.</p><p>QT spans ventricular depolarization and repolarization and varies with rate. Corrected QT formulas have limitations: Bazett tends to overcorrect at high rates and undercorrect at low rates. Interpret QTc with sex, rhythm, QRS duration, drugs and electrolytes.</p><ul><li><strong>Inferior:</strong> II, III, aVF.</li><li><strong>Lateral:</strong> I, aVL, V5–V6.</li><li><strong>Septal/anterior:</strong> V1–V4, with imperfect anatomical specificity.</li><li><strong>Posterior and right ventricular views:</strong> additional leads are needed when clinically indicated.</li></ul><p>Lead groups show electrical projections, not isolated slices of myocardium. Contiguous-lead changes, reciprocal patterns and clinical context are essential.</p>`,`The ECG studio displays a schematic rhythm strip. It cannot represent a complete 12-lead diagnostic examination or establish regional infarction.`),
L('Ischemic and nonischemic patterns',`<p>ST elevation has multiple causes, including acute coronary occlusion, pericarditis, early repolarization, ventricular aneurysm and some conduction patterns. Its significance depends on lead distribution, morphology, reciprocal change, symptoms, serial evolution and prior ECGs. A normal initial ECG does not exclude acute coronary syndrome.</p><p>ST depression may reflect subendocardial ischemia, reciprocal change, ventricular strain or drug effect. T-wave inversion is similarly nonspecific. Pathological Q waves may indicate prior infarction but are not synonymous with acute infarction. Posterior occlusion may manifest as anterior ST depression and requires targeted assessment.</p><p>Hyperkalemia may produce tall peaked T waves, PR prolongation and QRS widening, but ECG appearance is an unreliable quantitative potassium assay. Hypokalemia may produce ST depression, T flattening and U waves. Bundle branch blocks alter expected repolarization patterns.</p><p>Always separate <strong>description</strong> (“inferior ST elevation”) from <strong>interpretation</strong> (“possible acute inferior injury”) and the urgent clinical response.</p>`,`An unstable patient’s treatment should not wait for a perfectly named ECG pattern. Integrate pulse, blood pressure, symptoms and perfusion.`)
]},
{id:'arrhythmias',title:'Arrhythmias & conduction disorders',category:'Clinical',summary:'Narrow and broad tachycardias, AV block and the role of clinical stability.',objectives:['Distinguish AF, flutter, SVT and VT patterns.','Classify AV block without relying only on heart rate.','Explain why instability changes immediate priorities.'],sources:['ecg','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Supraventricular rhythms',`<p>Sinus tachycardia has organized sinus P waves and usually a physiological driver such as fever, pain, hypovolemia or anemia. Treating its cause is different from interrupting an arrhythmic circuit. AV nodal re-entry often produces a regular narrow-complex tachycardia with P waves hidden in or near QRS complexes.</p><p><strong>Atrial fibrillation</strong> has no consistent discrete P waves and an irregularly irregular ventricular response, unless AV block or pacing modifies the rhythm. AF management considers hemodynamic stability, rate/rhythm strategy, symptoms, underlying triggers and thromboembolic risk. Anticoagulation decisions require a validated risk assessment and bleeding/context review.</p><p><strong>Atrial flutter</strong> involves organized macro-re-entry with repetitive atrial activity. Fixed AV conduction can yield a regular ventricular rate; 2:1 conduction may hide flutter waves and be mistaken for another regular tachycardia.</p><p>A narrow QRS suggests ventricular activation via the normal conduction system but does not uniquely identify the mechanism. A supraventricular rhythm may be wide because of aberrancy or pre-excitation.</p>`,`Irregular broad-complex tachycardia with pre-excitation is a different problem from ordinary AF. Routine AV nodal blockers may be dangerous in pre-excited AF.`),
L('AV block and escape rhythms',`<p><strong>First-degree AV block:</strong> all P waves conduct, but PR is prolonged. <strong>Mobitz I:</strong> progressive PR prolongation culminates in a nonconducted P wave, followed by a shorter PR. <strong>Mobitz II:</strong> intermittent nonconducted P waves occur without progressive PR prolongation in conducted beats; disease is often infranodal.</p><p>A 2:1 block cannot always be assigned confidently to Mobitz I or II from a short strip alone. Inspect QRS width, longer recordings and clinical context. <strong>Complete AV block</strong> shows independent atrial and ventricular rhythms; the ventricular escape may be junctional and relatively narrow or distal and broad.</p><p>Sinus node dysfunction includes inappropriate bradycardia, pauses and tachy–brady patterns. A slow rate is not itself proof of disease; athletes, sleep and medications may explain physiological or reversible slowing.</p><p>Symptoms, perfusion, reversible causes and site of block guide urgency and pacing assessment. Drugs, ischemia, hyperkalemia and structural disease should be considered.</p>`,`AV dissociation describes atria and ventricles beating independently; it can also occur in VT and is not exclusively synonymous with complete AV block.`),
L('Ventricular rhythms and instability',`<p>A regular broad-complex tachycardia in an adult with structural heart disease should strongly raise concern for VT. AV dissociation, capture beats and fusion beats support VT, but their absence does not exclude it. Monomorphic VT often reflects a stable circuit; polymorphic VT has changing QRS morphology and may reflect several different mechanisms.</p><p>Torsades de pointes is a form of polymorphic VT associated with prolonged repolarization. Ventricular fibrillation is disorganized ventricular activity with no effective cardiac output. Pulseless VT and VF are shockable arrest rhythms; organized electrical activity without a pulse is PEA and requires a different resuscitation pathway.</p><p>Determine whether a pulse is present and whether the patient has shock, ischemic chest discomfort, acute heart failure or altered consciousness. <strong>Synchronized cardioversion</strong> and <strong>unsynchronized defibrillation</strong> serve different situations. Follow current resuscitation protocols under appropriate supervision rather than extrapolating from a teaching strip.</p>`,`“Wide complex” is a morphology description. It becomes a useful diagnosis only when combined with rhythm regularity, atrial activity, clinical history and stability.`)
]},
{id:'ischemic-heart-disease',title:'Ischemia & myocardial infarction',category:'Pathology',summary:'Supply–demand imbalance, plaque complications, necrosis and post-MI mechanical lesions.',objectives:['Distinguish injury, infarction and chronic ischemia.','Relate infarct evolution to complications.','Build a mechanism-based acute coronary assessment.'],sources:['physiology','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Atherosclerosis and ischemic syndromes',`<p>Atherosclerosis develops through endothelial dysfunction, retention/modification of lipoproteins, inflammation, foam-cell formation and fibrous plaque development. A lipid-rich inflamed plaque may rupture or erode, exposing thrombogenic material and triggering acute thrombosis. Severity of prior stable narrowing alone does not fully predict plaque instability.</p><p>Stable effort angina reflects a reproducible mismatch between demand and available coronary reserve, often with fixed obstructive disease. Acute coronary syndromes include unstable angina and myocardial infarction. With high-sensitivity troponin, many presentations previously termed unstable angina are recognized as infarction.</p><p><strong>Type 1 MI</strong> is associated with acute atherothrombotic plaque events. <strong>Type 2 MI</strong> arises from ischemic supply–demand imbalance without acute coronary atherothrombosis. Myocardial injury without evidence of ischemia is classified separately. Coronary vasospasm and microvascular dysfunction can cause ischemia without typical obstructive epicardial disease.</p>`,`Do not let a previously reassuring angiogram dismiss new symptoms. Coronary spasm, microvascular disease and new plaque events remain possible.`),
L('Evolution and complications of infarction',`<p>Persistent severe ischemia can progress from reversible dysfunction to coagulative necrosis. Histological appearances evolve from early injury to neutrophilic inflammation, macrophage clearance, granulation tissue and collagenous scar. Exact timing varies with reperfusion, infarct size and sampling; textbook day ranges are approximate.</p><p>Early complications include arrhythmias, acute pump failure and cardiogenic shock. During tissue softening in the first week, <strong>free-wall rupture</strong> may cause hemopericardium and tamponade; <strong>septal rupture</strong> produces an acute left-to-right shunt; <strong>papillary rupture</strong> causes acute MR. New hemodynamic deterioration after MI demands urgent reassessment.</p><p>Later complications include adverse remodeling, ventricular aneurysm, mural thrombus, chronic heart failure and scar-related VT. A true aneurysm contains thinned scarred ventricular wall. A pseudoaneurysm is a contained rupture and carries a different risk profile. Pericardial inflammation can occur early or as a delayed immune-mediated syndrome.</p>`,`A new murmur with pulmonary edema or shock after MI is a mechanical complication until urgently evaluated. Murmur loudness does not reliably measure severity.`),
L('Clinical reasoning and secondary prevention',`<p>Assessment integrates symptom onset and character, vital signs, examination, serial 12-lead ECGs and serial troponin interpreted using the assay’s validated pathway. Consider alternatives such as aortic dissection, pulmonary embolism, pericarditis and pneumothorax when the presentation suggests them.</p><p>Acute management depends on syndrome, timing, bleeding risk, hemodynamic state and local reperfusion capability. Antiplatelet therapy, anticoagulation and reperfusion each address different mechanisms; they are not interchangeable and have specific contraindications. Oxygen is targeted to hypoxemia or an appropriate clinical indication rather than routinely given to every normoxic patient.</p><p>Long-term risk reduction includes smoking cessation, lipid lowering, blood-pressure and diabetes management, activity/rehabilitation and indicated antithrombotic and ventricular-protective therapies. Medication choice depends on LV function, renal function, potassium, rhythm and comorbidity. Adherence and patient understanding determine whether an evidence-based plan works.</p>`,`Antithrombotic treatment appropriate for ACS may be harmful in another cause of chest pain. Clinical differentiation remains essential even when urgency is high.`)
]},
{id:'valvular-disease',title:'Valvular disease & murmur reasoning',category:'Pathology',summary:'Connect each lesion to timing, radiation, chamber loading and dynamic maneuvers.',objectives:['Compare the four major left-sided valve lesions.','Use dynamic maneuvers through their loading effects.','Distinguish acute from compensated chronic regurgitation.'],sources:['valves','anatomy'],lessons:[
L('Stenotic lesions: AS and MS',`<p><strong>Aortic stenosis</strong> obstructs LV ejection, increasing the LV-to-aortic systolic gradient and producing chronic pressure loading. Causes include calcific degeneration, bicuspid valve disease and rheumatic involvement. A harsh ejection systolic murmur is often best heard at the right upper sternal edge and radiates toward the carotids. Severe disease may produce a slow-rising low-volume pulse, exertional syncope, angina or dyspnea.</p><p><strong>Mitral stenosis</strong> obstructs diastolic LA-to-LV filling. LA pressure and size increase, predisposing to pulmonary venous hypertension and AF. Rheumatic disease is an important cause. Findings may include an opening snap and low-pitched apical mid-diastolic rumble. Presystolic accentuation requires atrial contraction and disappears in AF.</p><p>Echocardiography evaluates anatomy, gradients, valve area and downstream chamber effects. Gradients are flow-dependent: a low gradient does not automatically exclude important stenosis when stroke volume is low.</p>`,`An earlier opening snap after S2 can suggest higher LA pressure in mobile rheumatic MS, but heavily calcified immobile valves may have no audible snap.`),
L('Regurgitant lesions: MR and AR',`<p><strong>Mitral regurgitation</strong> permits LV-to-LA backflow during systole. Chronic disease allows LA and LV volume adaptation; acute severe MR abruptly raises LA/pulmonary venous pressure. The typical murmur is apical and holosystolic, often radiating to the axilla, but radiation varies with jet direction. Primary MR affects the valve apparatus; secondary MR reflects ventricular/atrial geometry and impaired coaptation.</p><p><strong>Aortic regurgitation</strong> permits aortic-to-LV flow during diastole. Chronic AR causes LV volume loading and may produce a wide pulse pressure, bounding pulses and an early diastolic decrescendo murmur. Acute AR may cause pulmonary edema and shock before these peripheral signs develop.</p><p>Regurgitation makes total stroke volume different from effective forward stroke volume. EF may look preserved despite impaired intrinsic function because ejection occurs partly into a lower-impedance chamber. Decisions require symptoms, severity and ventricular size/function, not murmur intensity alone.</p>`,`A “normal EF” can be misleading in significant MR. The measured EF includes regurgitant as well as forward ejection.`),
L('Dynamic maneuvers and integration',`<p>Use maneuvers by asking how they change venous return, chamber size or resistance. <strong>Inspiration</strong> increases right-sided filling and commonly augments right-sided murmurs, especially TR. <strong>Standing or Valsalva strain</strong> reduces LV volume; most flow murmurs decrease, while dynamic LV outflow obstruction can intensify and mitral prolapse clicks occur earlier.</p><p><strong>Squatting</strong> increases venous return and systemic resistance; dynamic obstruction commonly decreases. <strong>Isometric handgrip</strong> increases systemic resistance and can augment MR and AR, while effects on other lesions depend on loading and severity. These are helpful patterns, not infallible diagnostic laws.</p><p>A murmur description should include timing, duration, site, radiation, pitch, quality, intensity and response to maneuvers. Add pulse, JVP, heart sounds and signs of congestion. Echocardiography confirms anatomy and quantifies hemodynamics when clinically indicated.</p><p>A continuous murmur persists across S2; PDA is a classic cause. A to-and-fro systolic plus diastolic murmur is not necessarily truly continuous.</p>`,`A soft murmur can accompany a severe lesion when flow is low or pressures equalize rapidly. Grade the patient’s physiology as well as the sound.`)
]},
{id:'heart-failure',title:'Heart failure & cardiomyopathies',category:'Pathology',summary:'Congestion, perfusion, remodeling and systolic versus diastolic dysfunction.',objectives:['Explain why heart failure can occur with preserved EF.','Compare dilated, hypertrophic and restrictive phenotypes.','Connect neurohormonal responses with treatment targets.'],sources:['hf','physiology','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Syndrome and compensatory mechanisms',`<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome caused by structural or functional cardiac abnormality leading to elevated filling pressures, inadequate output or both. Dyspnea, fatigue and edema are interpreted with examination and objective cardiac evidence. Symptoms alone are not specific.</p><p>Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic pathways, renin–angiotensin–aldosterone signaling and vasopressin. Initially these support pressure and volume, but persistent activation increases loading, oxygen demand, sodium retention and adverse remodeling. Natriuretic peptides provide counter-regulatory signals and are useful biomarkers with clinical caveats.</p><p>Left-sided congestion raises pulmonary venous pressures and can cause orthopnea and pulmonary edema. Right-sided congestion produces elevated JVP, hepatic congestion and peripheral edema. Many patients have both. Distinguish <strong>congestion</strong> from <strong>hypoperfusion</strong>: a patient may be fluid-overloaded yet have low effective forward flow.</p>`,`Peripheral edema does not prove heart failure. Renal disease, liver disease, venous disease and medications are important alternatives.`),
L('EF phenotypes and cardiomyopathy',`<p>Common clinical EF categories are HFrEF at ≤40%, mildly reduced EF at 41–49%, and preserved EF at ≥50%; diagnosis of HFpEF additionally requires evidence compatible with elevated filling pressures/cardiac dysfunction. EF is a fraction and remains load-dependent.</p><p><strong>Dilated cardiomyopathy</strong> features chamber dilation with impaired systolic function. Causes include genetic disease, myocarditis, toxins and metabolic injury. <strong>Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> involves otherwise unexplained hypertrophy, often sarcomeric, with variable diastolic dysfunction, arrhythmic risk and dynamic obstruction. Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve may contribute to obstruction and MR.</p><p><strong>Restrictive cardiomyopathy</strong> impairs filling through myocardial stiffness; causes include infiltrative and storage diseases. Constrictive pericarditis can mimic restriction but arises outside the myocardium. Imaging, tissue characterization and hemodynamics help distinguish them.</p><p>Sudden death risk is individualized: wall thickness, scar, syncope, ventricular arrhythmia and family history may matter depending on the phenotype.</p>`,`Preserved radial squeeze does not guarantee normal relaxation, reserve or filling pressure. This is central to understanding HFpEF.`),
L('Therapeutic logic',`<p>Chronic HFrEF treatment targets maladaptive pathways as well as symptoms. The major disease-modifying groups include renin–angiotensin pathway therapy, often ARNI when appropriate; an evidence-based β-blocker; a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist; and an SGLT2 inhibitor. Choice and titration depend on hemodynamics, renal function, potassium and contraindications.</p><p>Loop diuretics relieve congestion; their symptomatic role should not be confused with the mortality evidence for foundational therapies. Device therapy, revascularization or valve intervention may be indicated for selected patients. HFpEF management includes treating congestion, comorbidities and indicated therapies with evidence for that phenotype.</p><p>Acute decompensation requires assessment of precipitating causes: ischemia, arrhythmia, infection, uncontrolled pressure, medication issues, renal dysfunction or mechanical lesions. Severe shock and pulmonary edema require monitored urgent care. A chronic outpatient regimen is not an acute resuscitation algorithm.</p>`,`A medication can improve long-term outcomes despite an initially negative inotropic effect. Stable initiation and appropriate monitoring are essential for β-blockers.`)
]},
{id:'congenital-disease',title:'Congenital heart disease',category:'Pathology',summary:'Shunts, cyanosis, obstructive lesions and Eisenmenger physiology.',objectives:['Predict shunt direction from pressure and resistance.','Distinguish ASD, VSD and PDA physiology.','Explain cyanotic lesions through their connections.'],sources:['embryology','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Left-to-right shunts',`<p>An ASD permits interatrial flow. In a typical uncomplicated lesion, greater RV compliance and lower right-sided filling resistance favor left-to-right flow, producing right-heart volume loading. A pulmonary flow murmur and fixed splitting of S2 can occur; the defect itself may not produce a loud murmur.</p><p>A VSD permits ventricular-level flow. A small restrictive defect can generate a loud pansystolic murmur because of a high pressure gradient, while a large nonrestrictive defect may have a lower gradient and greater pulmonary overcirculation. Lesion size, pulmonary resistance and associated obstruction determine effects.</p><p>PDA connects descending aorta to pulmonary artery. After pulmonary resistance falls, aortic-to-pulmonary flow may occur in both systole and diastole, producing a continuous murmur and volume loading of LA/LV. Very high pulmonary resistance changes this pattern.</p>`,`Murmur loudness and shunt magnitude can diverge. A small restrictive VSD may be louder than a large defect with near-equal ventricular pressures.`),
L('Cyanotic lesions and outflow obstruction',`<p><strong>Tetralogy of Fallot</strong> comprises RV outflow obstruction, VSD, overriding aorta and RV hypertrophy. Severity of outflow obstruction governs the degree of right-to-left shunting and cyanosis. The murmur is primarily from outflow obstruction, not necessarily the large VSD.</p><p><strong>Transposition of the great arteries</strong> creates parallel circulations. Survival requires mixing through a communication such as ASD, VSD or PDA. <strong>Truncus arteriosus</strong> involves a common arterial outlet with mixed blood and typically a VSD. Total anomalous pulmonary venous connection routes pulmonary venous return abnormally; obstruction markedly affects presentation.</p><p><strong>Coarctation</strong> obstructs aortic flow, often near the ductal insertion. Upper/lower limb pressure discrepancy and delayed femoral pulses are classic findings, but critically ill neonates may present differently. Duct-dependent lesions can deteriorate as the ductus closes.</p>`,`Use the anatomic connections to reconstruct the circulation before memorizing a disease label. This predicts whether the problem is mixing, obstruction or excessive pulmonary flow.`),
L('Pulmonary vascular remodeling and Eisenmenger',`<p>A longstanding substantial left-to-right shunt exposes the pulmonary circulation to excess flow and pressure. Endothelial injury and vascular remodeling can raise pulmonary vascular resistance. When resistance becomes sufficiently high, the shunt may become bidirectional or right-to-left, producing Eisenmenger physiology.</p><p>Consequences include cyanosis, secondary erythrocytosis, reduced exercise tolerance and complex bleeding/thrombotic risks. Closing an established shunt without assessing pulmonary vascular disease can remove a pressure-relief pathway and worsen outcomes. Operability is a specialist hemodynamic decision.</p><p>Shunt magnitude can be expressed as pulmonary-to-systemic flow ratio, Qp/Qs. Interpretation requires reliable oxygen saturations and an understanding of where blood mixes. A single peripheral saturation cannot characterize all lesions; differential cyanosis can occur when the shunt affects only part of the systemic circulation.</p><p>Congenital heart disease often requires lifelong follow-up even after repair, because residual lesions, arrhythmia, ventricular dysfunction and aortopathy may develop.</p>`,`“Repair completed” does not mean “normal anatomy and no future risk.” Learn the repaired circulation and its expected long-term complications.`)
]},
{id:'pericardium-shock',title:'Pericardial disease & shock',category:'Pathology',summary:'Pericarditis, tamponade, constriction and the hemodynamic patterns of shock.',objectives:['Distinguish inflammation from hemodynamic compression.','Explain ventricular interdependence and pulsus paradoxus.','Compare hypovolemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive shock.'],sources:['anatomy','physiology','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Acute pericarditis and effusion',`<p>Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium. Typical pain is pleuritic and positional, often improved by leaning forward, but presentations vary. A friction rub can be transient. Widespread ST elevation and PR depression are classic ECG findings, yet not universally present and not sufficient alone to exclude infarction.</p><p>Etiologies include idiopathic/viral syndromes, autoimmune disease, uremia, malignancy, tuberculosis and post-injury states. Evaluation is guided by clinical severity and epidemiology. Myocardial involvement changes the interpretation of troponin elevation, ventricular dysfunction and arrhythmic risk.</p><p>Effusion is fluid accumulation in the pericardial space and may occur with or without inflammation. The hemodynamic effect depends on rate of accumulation, volume and pericardial compliance. Echocardiography assesses fluid distribution and signs of chamber compression.</p><p>Large effusion, fever, subacute evolution, trauma, immunosuppression or suspected specific disease may require a different evaluation from uncomplicated low-risk pericarditis.</p>`,`A large cardiac silhouette cannot distinguish a dilated heart from pericardial fluid. Imaging must resolve the anatomy.`),
L('Tamponade and constriction',`<p><strong>Tamponade</strong> occurs when intrapericardial pressure limits cardiac filling and compromises output. Hypotension, raised JVP and muffled sounds form the classic Beck triad, but the triad is insensitive. Tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus and echocardiographic chamber collapse may provide clues in the appropriate setting.</p><p>Pulsus paradoxus is an exaggerated inspiratory fall in systolic pressure, conventionally greater than 10 mmHg. Under pericardial constraint, inspiratory RV filling can shift the septum and limit LV filling. It also occurs in severe obstructive airway disease and is not specific to tamponade.</p><p><strong>Constrictive pericarditis</strong> involves a rigid pericardial envelope that limits diastolic expansion. Early filling may be rapid but abruptly curtailed, with prominent ventricular interdependence and systemic venous congestion. Restrictive cardiomyopathy instead reflects myocardial stiffness; distinguishing them requires clinical, imaging and sometimes invasive evidence.</p>`,`Tamponade is a clinical-hemodynamic diagnosis supported by imaging. The presence of fluid alone does not establish it.`),
L('Shock through flow and resistance',`<p>Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion/oxygen utilization with potential cellular injury; hypotension is common but not its complete definition. Examine mentation, skin perfusion, urine output, lactate trends and the cause, recognizing each marker’s limitations.</p><ul><li><strong>Hypovolemic:</strong> reduced circulating volume limits preload and output; compensatory resistance usually rises.</li><li><strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> pump failure reduces output, often with elevated filling pressures, though patterns vary with the chamber involved.</li><li><strong>Distributive:</strong> pathological vasodilation and maldistribution lower systemic resistance; cardiac output may be high or low depending on stage and myocardial function.</li><li><strong>Obstructive:</strong> mechanical restriction to filling or ejection, as in tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism or tension pneumothorax.</li></ul><p>Mixed shock is common. A blind fluid strategy may worsen pulmonary edema or RV failure; treatment requires repeated assessment of response and the underlying mechanism.</p>`,`Low blood pressure is an observation. Before choosing an intervention, ask whether the dominant problem is volume, pump function, vascular tone or obstruction.`)
]},
{id:'infection-rheumatic',title:'Endocarditis, rheumatic disease & myocarditis',category:'Pathology',summary:'Infectious and immune injury across valves, myocardium and pericardium.',objectives:['Explain vegetation formation and embolic complications.','Connect rheumatic fever to chronic valve pathology.','Differentiate myocardial inflammation from ischemic injury.'],sources:['valves','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Infective endocarditis',`<p>Infective endocarditis involves microbial infection of endocardial surfaces, frequently valves or implanted material. Endothelial injury and platelet–fibrin deposition provide a substrate for adherent organisms; some pathogens can infect previously normal valves. Vegetations can destroy valve tissue, embolize and seed distant sites.</p><p>Presentation ranges from fulminant sepsis to prolonged fever, fatigue and weight loss. A new regurgitant lesion, embolic phenomena, conduction change or persistent bacteremia should prompt consideration. Peripheral findings are memorable but often absent. Prosthetic valves and cardiac devices create distinct diagnostic challenges.</p><p>Diagnosis integrates appropriately obtained blood cultures, echocardiography and accepted clinical criteria. Transesophageal imaging is more sensitive for many lesions and complications. Antibiotic selection and duration depend on organism, susceptibility, valve/device status and complications; microbiological sampling should precede antibiotics when clinically feasible without delaying necessary emergency care.</p>`,`New AV block in aortic valve endocarditis raises concern for extension into periannular tissue near the conduction system.`),
L('Acute rheumatic fever and chronic valve disease',`<p>Acute rheumatic fever is an immune-mediated complication of group A streptococcal infection. Diagnosis uses a clinical framework such as revised Jones criteria with evidence of preceding infection, accounting for population risk and exceptions. Carditis, migratory arthritis, chorea, erythema marginatum and subcutaneous nodules are classical major manifestations, but their prevalence differs.</p><p>Rheumatic carditis can involve multiple cardiac layers. Valve inflammation may cause acute regurgitation. Repeated or persistent injury leads to leaflet thickening, commissural fusion and shortening/fusion of chordae. Mitral involvement is common, often with aortic disease.</p><p>Chronic rheumatic MS raises LA pressure and creates risks of pulmonary hypertension, AF and thromboembolism. Prevention includes appropriate treatment of streptococcal infection and indicated secondary prophylaxis; regimens and duration depend on recurrence risk, age and cardiac involvement.</p>`,`Acute rheumatic valve dysfunction is often regurgitant. The classic fixed stenotic lesion usually reflects later scarring and remodeling.`),
L('Myocarditis and inflammatory cardiomyopathy',`<p>Myocarditis is myocardial inflammation with associated injury. Causes include infectious agents, immune-mediated disease and toxic/drug-related mechanisms. Presentations include chest pain mimicking ACS, arrhythmia, conduction disease, heart failure and cardiogenic shock.</p><p>Troponin may rise, ECG changes are variable and echocardiography may show global or regional dysfunction. Cardiac MRI can identify edema and patterns of injury/scar. Endomyocardial biopsy is reserved for selected situations in which establishing a specific diagnosis can change management; it is not a routine test for every mild presentation.</p><p>Management depends on hemodynamic stability, ventricular function, rhythm, etiology and specific inflammatory subtype. Some cases recover, while others progress to dilated cardiomyopathy or persistent arrhythmic substrate. Exercise restriction and return-to-activity assessment require individualized specialist guidance.</p><p>Myopericarditis and perimyocarditis describe overlapping inflammatory involvement with different emphasis on myocardial dysfunction; clinical severity matters more than terminology alone.</p>`,`A young age or recent viral illness does not make chest pain automatically benign. Myocarditis can produce important electrical and mechanical complications.`)
]},
{id:'pharmacology',title:'Cardiovascular pharmacology',category:'Pharmacology',summary:'Targets, mechanisms, useful effects, adverse effects and exam-level interactions.',objectives:['Explain drugs through receptors, ion channels and renal sites.','Compare disease-modifying and symptomatic effects.','Recognize important contraindications and monitoring needs.'],sources:['hf','physiology','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Hemodynamics and neurohormonal drugs',`<p><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> reduce angiotensin II generation and aldosterone signaling while increasing bradykinin. ARBs block AT₁ signaling without the same bradykinin effect. Important issues include renal function changes, hyperkalemia, pregnancy contraindication and ACE-inhibitor cough/angioedema. An ARNI combines neprilysin inhibition with angiotensin-receptor blockade; switching from an ACE inhibitor requires the recommended washout.</p><p><strong>β-blockers</strong> reduce adrenergic effects on rate, AV conduction and contractility; selected agents improve outcomes in stable HFrEF. Bradycardia, conduction block and bronchospasm risk depend on agent and patient. <strong>Dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers</strong> predominantly vasodilate; verapamil/diltiazem exert stronger cardiac nodal and negative inotropic effects.</p><p><strong>Nitrates</strong> generate nitric-oxide signaling, raising cGMP and relaxing vascular smooth muscle. Venodilation reduces preload; headache and hypotension are important effects. Concurrent phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor exposure can produce severe hypotension. Choice requires context, particularly in preload-sensitive states.</p>`,`Drug names that end similarly may share a mechanism, but selectivity, formulation, evidence and contraindications still differ.`),
L('Diuretics, inotropes and antiarrhythmics',`<p><strong>Loop diuretics</strong> inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, producing natriuresis; monitor volume, renal function, potassium and magnesium. <strong>Thiazides</strong> inhibit NCC in the distal convoluted tubule. <strong>Mineralocorticoid antagonists</strong> block aldosterone effects and can raise potassium. SGLT2 inhibitors have renal and cardiovascular effects beyond glucose lowering.</p><p><strong>Digoxin</strong> inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, altering sodium–calcium exchange and increasing intracellular calcium; vagal effects slow AV conduction. Renal impairment, interacting drugs and electrolyte disturbance increase toxicity risk. Symptoms and arrhythmias are more informative than assuming all nausea or ectopy is unrelated.</p><p>The Vaughan Williams framework: class I blocks fast sodium channels, II blocks β-receptors, III prolongs repolarization mainly through potassium-channel effects, and IV blocks L-type calcium channels with nodal effects. Adenosine, digoxin and magnesium do not fit neatly into these four classes. Amiodarone has multiple channel effects and important extracardiac toxicities.</p>`,`Antiarrhythmics can be proarrhythmic. Structural disease, QT, QRS width, electrolytes, renal function and interactions affect drug safety.`),
L('Antithrombotics and lipid therapy',`<p><strong>Antiplatelets</strong> target platelet activation: aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1/thromboxane production; P2Y₁₂ inhibitors reduce ADP-mediated activation. <strong>Anticoagulants</strong> target the coagulation cascade: heparins enhance antithrombin pathways, vitamin K antagonists reduce synthesis of vitamin K-dependent factors, and direct oral agents inhibit factor Xa or thrombin.</p><p>The indication matters. Platelet-rich arterial thrombosis, AF-related embolism, venous thromboembolism and mechanical valves are different contexts. Direct oral anticoagulants are not interchangeable with vitamin K antagonists for mechanical prosthetic valves. Combining antithrombotic drugs increases bleeding risk and requires a defined indication and duration.</p><p><strong>Statins</strong> inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, increasing hepatic LDL receptor expression. Ezetimibe reduces intestinal cholesterol uptake; PCSK9-directed therapies increase LDL receptor availability. Benefit is assessed against cardiovascular risk and achieved lipid reduction, with monitoring guided by symptoms, interactions and clinical context.</p>`,`“Blood thinner” obscures important distinctions. Be able to name whether a drug affects platelets, coagulation or fibrin breakdown.`)
]},
{id:'examination-investigations',title:'Bedside examination & investigations',category:'Clinical',summary:'History, JVP, pulse, auscultation, echo, hemodynamics and a viva-ready synthesis.',objectives:['Present a focused cardiovascular examination.','Choose investigations to answer specific clinical questions.','Build differential diagnoses from physiology rather than isolated signs.'],sources:['anatomy','ecg','valves','guidelines'],lessons:[
L('Focused history and examination',`<p>Characterize chest discomfort by onset, duration, location, radiation, provoking/relieving factors and associated symptoms. For breathlessness, establish exercise tolerance, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and competing pulmonary causes. Syncope requires posture, exertion, prodrome, recovery, injury, medication and family history; exertional syncope or sudden-death history deserves particular attention.</p><p>Examine general appearance and perfusion before individual signs. Assess pulse rate, rhythm, character and symmetry; measure blood pressure appropriately and consider orthostatic or limb comparisons when indicated. Estimate JVP with appropriate positioning, distinguishing venous from arterial pulsation.</p><p>Inspect and palpate the precordium for apex location, heaves and thrills. Auscultate S1/S2, additional sounds and murmurs with suitable positioning, diaphragm/bell use and dynamic maneuvers. Examine lungs, edema, liver and peripheral signs to integrate congestion and systemic effects.</p>`,`A useful presentation links findings: “Raised JVP, edema and hepatomegaly indicate systemic venous congestion,” then explains the likely cardiac or noncardiac cause.`),
L('Investigations and their questions',`<p><strong>ECG:</strong> what rhythm, conduction or electrical injury pattern is present? <strong>Echocardiography:</strong> what are chamber size/function, valve structure and gradients, regional motion and pericardial findings? Doppler velocity estimates require alignment and assumptions; the simplified Bernoulli relation ΔP ≈ 4v² estimates a pressure gradient in mmHg when velocity is m/s.</p><p><strong>Chest radiography:</strong> assess heart silhouette, pulmonary congestion and alternatives, recognizing projection effects. <strong>Troponin:</strong> establish myocardial injury and change over time. <strong>Natriuretic peptides:</strong> support evaluation of suspected heart failure, with effects from age, kidney disease, AF and obesity.</p><p>Coronary CT, invasive angiography, stress testing and cardiac MRI answer different questions about anatomy, inducible ischemia, tissue characterization and risk. Right-heart catheterization can assess pressures, output and pulmonary vascular resistance when noninvasive tests are insufficient or specific decisions require it.</p>`,`A test should answer a question. “Order everything cardiac” adds noise; specify the suspected mechanism and what finding would change your interpretation.`),
L('Clinical synthesis and viva framework',`<p>Summarize the patient’s syndrome, functional impact, dominant hemodynamics and likely lesion. Then state severity, complications and important alternatives. For a valve case: identify the lesion, explain its pressure/volume effect, list supporting signs, and name the investigation that establishes severity.</p><p>For a long-case presentation, organize reasoning as <strong>etiology → structural change → physiological disturbance → symptoms/signs → investigation → management principles</strong>. Distinguish confirmed findings from hypotheses. For example, breathlessness plus a diastolic apical murmur suggests MS, but severity, rhythm, pulmonary pressure and embolic risk require assessment.</p><p>Useful viva prompts: Why does AF worsen MS symptoms? Why can tamponade occur with a small effusion? Why does an enlarged LV consume more oxygen? Why does increasing afterload widen ESV? Why can EF remain normal in MR? Answer each using pressure gradients, geometry or cellular mechanisms before listing associations.</p><p>Close the learning loop by explaining one unfamiliar finding, identifying an uncertainty and checking an appropriate reference. Recall and application reveal gaps that rereading alone may hide.</p>`,`A strong clinical answer makes its reasoning inspectable. State the mechanism and the evidence that would support or challenge it.`)
]}
];

modules.forEach((module,index)=>{
  module.number=String(index+1).padStart(2,'0');
  module.lessons.forEach((lesson,i)=>{lesson.id=`${module.id}-${i+1}`;});
});

const Q=(category,prompt,options,answer,explanation)=>({category,prompt,options,answer,explanation});
export const questions=[
Q('Anatomy','Which chamber forms most of the sternocostal surface of the heart?',['Left atrium','Left ventricle','Right ventricle','Right atrium'],2,'The RV lies most anteriorly. The LV forms the apex and most of the left border; the LA forms most of the posterior base.'),
Q('Anatomy','Coronary dominance is defined by the origin of which artery?',['SA nodal artery','Posterior descending artery','Left anterior descending artery','Largest obtuse marginal branch'],1,'Dominance refers to the arterial source of the posterior descending artery. It is not determined by coronary size or SA nodal supply.'),
Q('Anatomy','What is the main mechanical role of papillary muscles during systole?',['Open the AV valves','Open the semilunar valves','Prevent AV leaflet prolapse','Generate atrial contraction'],2,'Papillary muscles tension chordae during systole. Pressure gradients move the leaflets; the muscles help prevent eversion into the atria.'),
Q('Anatomy','Why is the posteromedial LV papillary muscle particularly vulnerable to ischemic rupture?',['It has no chordal attachment','It generally has a single principal arterial supply','It is exclusively supplied by LAD','It contracts only during diastole'],1,'The posteromedial group generally receives one principal arterial supply, whereas the anterolateral group more often has supply from both LAD and LCx branches.'),
Q('Anatomy','Pericardial irritation produces shoulder pain through which spinal roots?',['C3–C5','T1–T5 only','T7–T10','L1–L3'],0,'The phrenic nerve supplies somatic sensation to the fibrous and parietal serous pericardium. Its C3–C5 origin explains shoulder referral.'),
Q('Foundations','Which structure connects myocardium electrically from cell to cell?',['Desmosomes alone','Gap junctions','Chordae tendineae','Fibrous valve annulus'],1,'Gap junctions allow ionic current between adjacent cells. Adherens junctions and desmosomes primarily transmit mechanical force.'),
Q('Foundations','Which fetal shunt directs blood from pulmonary trunk toward descending aorta?',['Foramen ovale','Ductus venosus','Ductus arteriosus','Coronary sinus'],2,'The ductus arteriosus bypasses the high-resistance fetal lungs. The foramen ovale links atria; ductus venosus bypasses part of the hepatic circulation.'),
Q('Foundations','What promotes functional closure of the foramen ovale after birth?',['RA pressure exceeds LA pressure','Pulmonary resistance rises sharply','LA pressure exceeds RA pressure','The ductus venosus becomes an artery'],2,'Pulmonary blood flow and LA return rise after ventilation, while systemic changes reduce fetal right-sided streaming. LA pressure presses the septum primum flap against septum secundum.'),
Q('Physiology','A ventricle has EDV 130 mL, ESV 50 mL and HR 75/min. What is cardiac output?',['3.75 L/min','6.0 L/min','9.75 L/min','80 L/min'],1,'SV = 130 − 50 = 80 mL. CO = 80 × 75 = 6,000 mL/min = 6.0 L/min.'),
Q('Physiology','At fixed EDV and contractility, an acute increase in effective arterial elastance most directly causes:',['Lower ESV and higher SV','Higher ESV and lower SV','No change in ESV','Complete disappearance of diastole'],1,'The higher opposing load shifts end systole toward a larger residual volume. This lowers stroke volume in the simplified normal-valve coupling model.'),
Q('Physiology','Which variable is approximately represented by the width of a PV loop?',['End-diastolic pressure','Cardiac output','Stroke volume','Ejection fraction directly'],2,'Horizontal width is EDV minus ESV, or stroke volume. Loop area corresponds approximately to external stroke work.'),
Q('Physiology','Reduced ventricular compliance means that at the same end-diastolic volume:',['Filling pressure is lower','Filling pressure is higher','Ejection fraction must be zero','Heart rate must double'],1,'A stiffer ventricle requires higher pressure to accommodate the same volume; the EDPVR shifts upward/leftward.'),
Q('Physiology','Which event terminates isovolumetric contraction?',['Mitral valve opening','Aortic valve opening','Aortic valve closure','P wave onset'],1,'As LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and ejection begins. During the preceding isovolumetric phase, both valves are closed.'),
Q('Physiology','Why is an S4 absent in atrial fibrillation?',['There is no ventricular filling','There is no organized atrial contraction','Aortic valve never closes','Ventricular stiffness always normalizes'],1,'S4 is associated with atrial contraction into a stiff ventricle. AF removes coordinated atrial systole, even if the ventricle remains stiff.'),
Q('Physiology','What principally produces the phase 0 upstroke in AV nodal cells?',['Fast sodium current','L-type calcium current','Chloride efflux','SERCA pumping'],1,'Nodal cells have a calcium-dependent slow-response upstroke. Working ventricular myocytes use fast sodium channels for phase 0.'),
Q('Physiology','Which change most directly reduces LV coronary perfusion pressure?',['Higher aortic diastolic pressure','Lower LV end-diastolic pressure','Higher LV end-diastolic pressure','Longer diastole'],2,'A useful approximation is aortic diastolic pressure minus LVEDP. Raising LVEDP reduces this driving gradient, particularly affecting subendocardium.'),
Q('Clinical','At 25 mm/s, how much time does one small horizontal ECG square represent?',['4 ms','20 ms','40 ms','200 ms'],2,'25 mm pass per second, so 1 mm represents 1/25 second = 40 ms. A 5-mm large square is 200 ms.'),
Q('Clinical','Progressive PR prolongation followed by a dropped QRS most strongly suggests:',['First-degree AV block','Mobitz I AV block','Mobitz II AV block','Sinus arrest'],1,'Wenckebach periodicity involves increasing PR intervals before a nonconducted P wave. Mobitz II has stable conducted PR intervals before intermittent block.'),
Q('Clinical','A rhythm is irregularly irregular without consistent P waves. Which is most likely?',['Sinus tachycardia','Atrial fibrillation','Regular AVNRT','Monomorphic VT in every case'],1,'AF is characterized by disorganized atrial activation and an irregular ventricular response in ordinary AV conduction. Artifact and other rhythms still require exclusion.'),
Q('Clinical','Inferior myocardial electrical changes are assessed primarily in:',['I, aVL, V5, V6','V1 and V2 only','II, III, aVF','aVR only'],2,'II, III and aVF view the inferior territory. These projections do not uniquely establish a culprit coronary artery because anatomy varies.'),
Q('Clinical','In an older patient with prior MI, a regular broad-complex tachycardia should strongly raise concern for:',['Physiological sinus arrhythmia','Ventricular tachycardia','Isolated first-degree AV block','A normal variant'],1,'Structural scar provides a substrate for VT. Treating a broad tachycardia as benign supraventricular rhythm without adequate assessment can be dangerous.'),
Q('Pathology','A small VSD may have a louder murmur than a large VSD because:',['Small defects always carry more flow','A restrictive defect can create a larger pressure gradient','Large defects cannot shunt','Murmur intensity is unrelated to turbulence'],1,'A small restrictive orifice often has high-velocity flow driven by a large gradient. Larger nonrestrictive defects can equalize pressures despite carrying a substantial shunt.'),
Q('Pathology','A new apical systolic murmur and pulmonary edema several days after MI suggest which mechanical lesion?',['Papillary muscle rupture with acute MR','Isolated chronic AS','Small asymptomatic ASD','Physiological S3 only'],0,'Ischemic rupture disrupts the mitral apparatus. Acute regurgitation abruptly raises LA and pulmonary venous pressure; urgent echocardiographic assessment is needed.'),
Q('Pathology','Why can a small acute pericardial effusion cause tamponade?',['All effusions cause tamponade','The pericardium has no vessels','Rapid accumulation exceeds its ability to stretch','Small effusions always contain gas'],2,'Pressure rises steeply when fluid accumulates faster than the pericardium can adapt. Volume alone does not determine hemodynamic severity.'),
Q('Pathology','Which lesion classically produces a fixed wide split S2?',['Uncomplicated secundum ASD','Isolated LV hypertrophy','Pure acute MR','Complete absence of RV filling'],0,'ASD-related right-sided volume loading and respiratory changes in shunting reduce the normal respiratory variation in splitting.'),
Q('Pathology','An ejection systolic murmur radiates to the carotids with a slow-rising pulse. Which lesion fits best?',['Mitral stenosis','Aortic stenosis','Aortic regurgitation','Tricuspid regurgitation'],1,'Aortic outflow obstruction produces an ejection murmur with carotid radiation and can delay arterial upstroke. Echo establishes severity and flow dependence.'),
Q('Pathology','Why can preserved EF coexist with heart failure?',['EF measures all aspects of diastolic function','A stiff ventricle can have high filling pressures despite preserved fractional emptying','Preserved EF proves cardiac output is high','Heart failure never requires objective evidence'],1,'HFpEF involves abnormal filling/relaxation and elevated filling pressures. EF does not measure all cardiac function and can remain preserved when stroke volume is limited.'),
Q('Pathology','A markedly elevated troponin establishes which finding directly?',['Plaque rupture in every patient','Myocardial injury','A uniquely LAD territory lesion','A normal myocardium'],1,'Troponin identifies myocardial injury. Acute MI additionally requires evidence of ischemia in the appropriate rise/fall and clinical context.'),
Q('Pharmacology','Which drug mechanism increases cGMP-mediated vascular relaxation?',['Nitrate-derived nitric oxide','β₁ receptor antagonism alone','Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase stimulation','P2Y₁₂ activation'],0,'Nitrates generate NO signaling, activating soluble guanylyl cyclase and increasing cGMP. Combining them with PDE5 inhibitors can cause severe hypotension.'),
Q('Pharmacology','Loop diuretics act primarily on which transporter?',['NCC in the distal convoluted tubule','NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb','SGLT1 in the gut','ENaC only in the collecting duct'],1,'Loop diuretics inhibit the sodium–potassium–2 chloride cotransporter, producing potent natriuresis. Electrolyte and renal/volume monitoring are important.'),
Q('Pharmacology','Why does hypokalemia increase susceptibility to digoxin toxicity?',['It prevents digoxin from binding','It promotes digoxin interaction with Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase','It eliminates all calcium currents','It accelerates renal clearance in every patient'],1,'Potassium and digoxin interactions at Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase help explain increased sensitivity in hypokalemia. Renal impairment and interacting drugs also raise risk.'),
Q('Pharmacology','Which distinction between antiplatelets and anticoagulants is correct?',['Both act only on fibrin breakdown','Antiplatelets target platelet activation; anticoagulants target coagulation pathways','Anticoagulants are always superior in arterial disease','They can always be combined without extra risk'],1,'They act on different parts of thrombosis. The lesion, indication, bleeding risk and evidence determine the correct agent or combination.'),
Q('Physiology','Which quantity can be normal despite severe anemia and reduced oxygen delivery?',['Hemoglobin concentration','Pulse oximeter saturation','Arterial oxygen content in every case','Total oxygen delivery in every case'],1,'SpO₂ estimates hemoglobin saturation, not total oxygen content. A smaller amount of fully saturated hemoglobin still carries less oxygen.'),
Q('Clinical','Aortic stenosis severity cannot be excluded solely by a low Doppler gradient because:',['Doppler never measures velocity','The gradient is flow-dependent','All gradients are unrelated to pressure','Severe AS always has a normal valve area'],1,'Low stroke volume can produce a low gradient despite important valve obstruction. Integrate valve area, flow, LV function and dedicated assessment.'),
Q('Foundations','Which change improves relaxation during β-adrenergic stimulation?',['Permanent SERCA inhibition','Phospholamban phosphorylation relieving SERCA inhibition','Increased cytosolic calcium forever','Complete loss of ATP consumption'],1,'Faster SR calcium reuptake reduces cytosolic calcium sooner, supporting positive lusitropy while increasing SR calcium available for the next beat.'),
Q('Pathology','In Eisenmenger physiology, why can indiscriminate shunt closure be dangerous?',['It always causes mitral stenosis','It may remove a decompression pathway in severe pulmonary vascular disease','It always increases lung oxygenation','Pulmonary resistance must be normal'],1,'Advanced pulmonary vascular disease alters operability. Eliminating the communication can worsen right-sided hemodynamics; specialist assessment is necessary.')
];
questions.forEach((q,i)=>q.id=`q${i+1}`);

